The predicate that decides a home-and-office commuter-exclusion preview is out of date for the current policy is duplicated across two files. Here `isCommuterExclusionPreviewStale` computes `method === HOME_AND_OFFICE && hasRoute && !routeError && transaction?.commuterExclusionPreview?.policyID !== policy.id`, and `useDistanceRequestState.ts` (`isCommuterExclusionPreviewPending`) recomputes the same core condition. If the definition of "stale/pending preview" changes, both copies must be kept in sync, which is easy to miss.

Extract the shared check into a single helper (e.g. in `DistanceRequestUtils` or `TransactionUtils`) and call it from both hooks:

```ts
// DistanceRequestUtils.ts
function hasStaleCommuterExclusionPreview(transaction: OnyxEntry<Transaction>, policy: OnyxEntry<Policy>): boolean {
    return (
        policy?.commuterExclusions?.method === CONST.POLICY.COMMUTER_EXCLUSION_METHOD.HOME_AND_OFFICE &&
        hasRoute(transaction) &&
        !transaction?.errorFields?.route &&
        transaction?.commuterExclusionPreview?.policyID !== policy.id
    );
}
```

Then reuse it in both `useFetchRoute` (to gate the fetch) and `useDistanceRequestState` (to gate the pending UI state).

This selection-item report resolution (`const report = ('report' in item ? item.report : undefined) ?? allReports?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT}${item.reportID}`];`) is duplicated verbatim from the `doAllSelectedItemsBelongToCADPolicies` memo just above (it resolves the report the same way, including the same explanatory comment). Duplicating this lookup in two adjacent memos means any future change to how a selection row resolves its report has to be made in two places.

Extract a small shared helper and reuse it in both memos, e.g.:

```ts
const resolveSelectionReport = (item: SelectionItem) =>
    ('report' in item ? item.report : undefined) ?? allReports?.[`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.REPORT}${item.reportID}`];

// in canExportReconciliationAllExpenses:
const report = resolveSelectionReport(item);
const reportType = ('type' in item ? item.type : undefined) ?? report?.type;
return reportType !== CONST.REPORT.TYPE.IOU;
```

This enrollment-routing block duplicates logic that already lives in `WorkspaceExpensifyCardPageEmptyState.tsx`. The `eligibleBankAccounts` computation here (`isUkEuCurrencySupported ? getEligibleBankAccountsForUkEuCard(bankAccountList, supportedCountriesByCurrency, ...) : getEligibleBankAccountsForCard(bankAccountList)`) is identical to lines 89-91 there, and the `!eligibleBankAccounts.length || hasInProgressUSDVBBA(reimbursementAccount?.achData)` decision that routes to `BANK_ACCOUNT_WITH_STEP_TO_OPEN` (with `backTo: WORKSPACE_EXPENSIFY_CARD`) versus the choose-bank-account route mirrors `shouldStartBankAccountSetup` / `startFlow` in that file. The only real difference is the currency source (the just-selected `item.currencyCode` versus the persisted `policy.outputCurrency`), which can be parameterized.

Extract this into a shared helper (e.g. in `@libs/CardUtils`) that both call sites reuse, so the eligible-account computation and the enrollment route decision live in one place:

```ts
// CardUtils.ts
function getExpensifyCardEnrollmentRoute(
    policyID: string,
    currencyCode: string,
    isUkEuCurrencySupported: boolean,
    bankAccountList: OnyxEntry<BankAccountList>,
    supportedCountriesByCurrency: OnyxEntry<CardSupportedCountries>,
    achData: OnyxEntry<ReimbursementAccount>['achData'],
) {
    const eligibleBankAccounts = isUkEuCurrencySupported
        ? getEligibleBankAccountsForUkEuCard(bankAccountList, supportedCountriesByCurrency, currencyCode)
        : getEligibleBankAccountsForCard(bankAccountList);
    if (!eligibleBankAccounts.length || hasInProgressUSDVBBA(achData)) {
        return ROUTES.BANK_ACCOUNT_WITH_STEP_TO_OPEN.getRoute({policyID, backTo: ROUTES.WORKSPACE_EXPENSIFY_CARD.getRoute(policyID)});
    }
    return ROUTES.WORKSPACE_EXPENSIFY_CARD_BANK_ACCOUNT.getRoute(policyID);
}
```

Then both this page and `WorkspaceExpensifyCardPageEmptyState.tsx` navigate to the returned route.

This `handleDelete` is a near-verbatim copy of the one added to `FlagForReviewRulePageBase.tsx` in this same PR: the same `showConfirmModal({...})` config (same `title`, `prompt`, `confirmText`, `cancelText`, `buttonVariant`), the same `.then((result) => { if (result.action !== ModalActions.CONFIRM) return; ...; Navigation.goBack(); })` shape, and even the same explanatory comment. Only the delete call itself differs (`deleteRequireFieldsRule(...)` vs `deleteFlagForReviewRule(...)`). Since the PR already extracts the shared header into `useRuleDeleteHeaderProps`, the confirm-modal + delete + goBack scaffolding should be consolidated too.

Extract the shared prompt-and-delete flow into a helper (or fold it into `useRuleDeleteHeaderProps`) and inject only the per-rule delete callback, e.g.:

```ts
function useRuleDeleteConfirm(onConfirmDelete: () => void) {
    const {translate} = useLocalize();
    const {showConfirmModal} = useConfirmModal();
    return () =>
        showConfirmModal({
            title: translate('workspace.rules.merchantRules.deleteRule'),
            prompt: translate('workspace.rules.merchantRules.deleteRuleConfirmation'),
            confirmText: translate('common.delete'),
            cancelText: translate('common.cancel'),
            buttonVariant: CONST.BUTTON_VARIANT.DANGER,
        }).then((result) => {
            if (result.action !== ModalActions.CONFIRM) {
                return;
            }
            onConfirmDelete();
            Navigation.goBack();
        });
}
```

Then each page passes only its own delete call (`() => deleteRequireFieldsRule(policyData, getRequireFieldsRuleKey(categoryName))`).

This change replaces a single, data-driven icon list with ten near-identical `<Icon>` JSX blocks that differ only in the guarding boolean and the `src` value. Every block repeats the same `size={CONST.ICON_SIZE.SMALL}` and `additionalStyles={[integrationIconStyle]}` structure, so adding, removing, or restyling an integration icon now requires editing every copy, raising maintenance overhead and bug risk (DRY violation).

Consolidate the repetition by iterating over a small config that maps a condition to its icon source, keeping a single `<Icon>` definition:

```tsx
const integrationIcons = [
    {show: isExportedToNetsuite, name: 'NetSuiteSquare', src: icons.NetSuiteSquare},
    {show: isExportedToXero, name: 'XeroSquare', src: icons.XeroSquare},
    {show: isExportedToIntacct, name: 'IntacctSquare', src: icons.IntacctSquare},
    {show: isExportedToQuickbooksOnline || isExportedToQuickbooksDesktop, name: 'QBOSquare', src: icons.QBOSquare},
    {show: isExportedToIntuitEnterpriseSuite, name: 'IntuitSquare', src: icons.IntuitSquare},
    {show: isExportedToCertinia, name: 'CertiniaSquare', src: icons.CertiniaSquare},
    {show: isExportedToRillet, name: 'RilletSquare', src: icons.RilletSquare},
    {show: isExportedToDualEntry, name: 'DualEntrySquare', src: icons.DualEntrySquare},
    {show: isExportedToBillCom, name: 'BillComSquare', src: icons.BillComSquare},
    {show: isExportedToZenefits, name: 'ZenefitsSquare', src: icons.ZenefitsSquare},
];

// ...
{integrationIcons
    .filter(({show}) => show)
    .map(({name, src}) => (
        <Icon
            key={name}
            src={src}
            size={CONST.ICON_SIZE.SMALL}
            additionalStyles={[integrationIconStyle]}
        />
    ))}
```

This keeps a single source of truth for the `<Icon>` markup while still avoiding the removed inline object churn.

This new `focusAddressLineOnPrivatePersonalDetailsLinks` helper is a second, inconsistent implementation of the same operation the code already performs one call earlier: rewriting an anchor `href` inside the very same report action HTML. It is chained directly onto `adjustExpensifyLinksForEnv` (from `@components/EnvironmentContextProvider`), which rewrites hrefs in this HTML too. That existing helper deliberately edits the raw HTML string with a regex and documents *why* (lines 32-43 of `EnvironmentContextProvider/index.tsx`): a round trip through an HTML parser/serializer corrupts the App's custom tags (it mangles `<mention-user>`, lowercases attributes MentionUserRenderer relies on, and converts emoji to numeric character references). Introducing a `htmlparser2` `parseDocument`/`DomUtils.findAll` pass over the same content re-solves a solved problem with the exact approach the neighboring code was written to avoid, and now two different mechanisms must be kept in sync for the same href-rewriting concern.

Reuse the existing regex-based anchor-rewriting pattern instead of parsing. For example, fold the address-focus rewrite into the same raw-string approach already used in `EnvironmentContextProvider`:

```tsx
const ANCHOR_HREF_REGEX = /(\shref\s*=\s*")([^"]*)"/gi;

function focusAddressLineOnPrivatePersonalDetailsLinks(html: string): string {
    if (!html || !html.includes(PRIVATE_PERSONAL_DETAILS_ROUTE)) {
        return html;
    }

    return html.replaceAll(ANCHOR_HREF_REGEX, (match, prefix: string, href: string) => {
        const [path] = href.split('?');
        if (!path.endsWith(PRIVATE_PERSONAL_DETAILS_ROUTE)) {
            return match;
        }
        const newHref = `${path.slice(0, -PRIVATE_PERSONAL_DETAILS_ROUTE.length)}${PRIVATE_PERSONAL_DETAILS_ROUTE_WITH_FOCUS}`;
        return `${prefix}${newHref}"`;
    });
}
```

Better still, consider extending `adjustExpensifyLinksForEnv` (or a shared report-action-HTML link helper) so both rewrites live in one place rather than as two separate passes.

This `returnToOriginReport` closure (`dismissOnboardingModalBeforeExit()` followed by `Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.REPORT_WITH_ID.getRoute(originReportID ?? conciergeReportID))`) is duplicated verbatim across three onboarding screens: here, `BaseOnboardingWorkspaces.tsx`, and `BaseOnboardingWorkEmail.tsx`. Duplicated navigation logic drifts out of sync and must be fixed in every copy.

Extract the shared "return to the report the task was opened from" behavior into a single reusable hook (e.g. `useReturnToOriginReport`) and consume it in all three screens:

```tsx
// src/hooks/useReturnToOriginReport.ts
function useReturnToOriginReport() {
    const [originReportID] = useState(() => Navigation.getTopmostReportId());
    const [conciergeReportID] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.CONCIERGE_REPORT_ID);
    return useCallback(() => {
        dismissOnboardingModalBeforeExit();
        Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.REPORT_WITH_ID.getRoute(originReportID ?? conciergeReportID));
    }, [originReportID, conciergeReportID]);
}
```

The approver-label expression `${toLocaleOrdinalWithWords(1)} ${translate('common.approver').toLowerCase()}` (falling back to `translate('common.approver')`) is duplicated here and in `src/components/Tables/WorkspaceMembersTable/index.tsx`. Both files build the exact same "First approver" / "Approver" copy, differing only in the multi-approver condition. Keeping the copy logic in two places risks the two labels drifting apart.

Extract a small shared helper, e.g. in `WorkflowUtils` or a localize helper, and call it from both sites:

```ts
// WorkflowUtils.ts
function getApproverLabel(hasMultipleApprovers: boolean, translate: LocaleContextProps['translate'], toLocaleOrdinalWithWords: LocaleContextProps['toLocaleOrdinalWithWords']): string {
    return hasMultipleApprovers ? `${toLocaleOrdinalWithWords(1)} ${translate('common.approver').toLowerCase()}` : translate('common.approver');
}
```

Then: `const approverLabel = getApproverLabel((memberApprovalWorkflow?.approvers.length ?? 0) > 1, translate, toLocaleOrdinalWithWords);`

The "first approver" vs "approver" label derivation is duplicated here and in `src/components/Tables/WorkspaceMembersTable/index.tsx` (line 117). Both build ``${toLocaleOrdinalWithWords(1)} ${translate(<key>).toLowerCase()}`` when there is more than one approver, and fall back to the plain `Approver` translation otherwise — differing only by the translation key.

Extract a shared helper so the ordinal/lowercasing logic lives in one place, e.g.:

```ts
function getApproverLabel(hasMultipleApprovers: boolean, translate: LocaleContextProps['translate'], toLocaleOrdinalWithWords: LocaleContextProps['toLocaleOrdinalWithWords'], approverKey: TranslationPaths) {
    return hasMultipleApprovers ? `${toLocaleOrdinalWithWords(1)} ${translate(approverKey).toLowerCase()}` : translate(approverKey);
}
```

and call it from both sites.

This expression that counts a report's non-null `errorFields.export` entries — `Object.values(report?.errorFields?.export ?? {}).filter((error) => error != null).length` — is duplicated verbatim in `src/libs/ReportUtils.ts` inside `isExportInProgress` (where it computes `exportErrorCount`). Both compute the same value for the same purpose (identifying a new export error against a snapshotted baseline). If the shape of the export-error count ever changes (e.g. the null-filtering rule), it must be kept in sync in two places, which is exactly the drift risk DRY guards against.

Extract a small shared helper in `ReportUtils.ts` and call it from both sites:

```ts
// ReportUtils.ts
function getExportErrorCount(report: OnyxEntry<Report>): number {
    return Object.values(report?.errorFields?.export ?? {}).filter((error) => error != null).length;
}
```

Then use `getExportErrorCount(report)` here and inside `isExportInProgress`.

These `getCurrencyDecimals`, `getCurrencySymbol`, `convertToDisplayString`, and `convertToDisplayStringWithoutCurrency` implementations are copied verbatim from `CurrencyListContextProvider` (`src/components/CurrencyListContextProvider/index.tsx`). The exact same `convertToDisplayString` body is now also duplicated in `src/libs/Notification/LocalNotification/BrowserNotifications.ts`, so this formatting logic exists in three places. The `2` decimals cap, the `sanitizeCurrencyCode` + `getCurrencyDecimals` + `convertToFrontendAmountAsInteger` + `format`/`formatToParts` pipeline, and the part-filtering are all repeated. When the formatting rules change, every copy must be updated in lockstep.

Extract the standalone (non-React) formatters into a shared helper in `CurrencyUtils` that takes `currencyList` and `locale` as arguments, and have both the provider and these non-React call paths (this file and `BrowserNotifications.ts`) call it:

```ts
// CurrencyUtils.ts
function buildConvertToDisplayString(currencyList: CurrencyList, locale: Locale): CurrencyListActionsContextType['convertToDisplayString'] {
    return (amountInCents, currencyCode) => {
        const sanitizedCurrency = sanitizeCurrencyCode(currencyCode);
        const decimals = currencyList?.[sanitizedCurrency]?.decimals ?? CONST.DEFAULT_CURRENCY_DECIMALS;
        const convertedAmount = convertToFrontendAmountAsInteger(amountInCents ?? 0, decimals);
        return format(locale, convertedAmount, {style: 'currency', currency: sanitizedCurrency, minimumFractionDigits: decimals, maximumFractionDigits: 2});
    };
}
```

Then here: `const convertToDisplayString = buildConvertToDisplayString(currencyList ?? {}, preferredLocale);`

The `debitedamount` and `creditedamount` cases are near-identical: both guard on a `<x>Amount`/`<x>Currency` pair, call `formatAmount`, and return `formattedAmount ?? part.definition`. The only difference is which pair of report fields is read, so the logic can be consolidated to avoid duplication.

Extract a small helper and call it from both cases:

```ts
const formatReimbursementAmount = (amount: number | undefined, currency: string | undefined): string => {
    if (!amount || !currency) {
        return '';
    }
    return formatAmount(amount, currency, format, context.getCurrencyDecimals) ?? part.definition;
};

case 'debitedamount':
    return formatReimbursementAmount(report.debitedAmount, report.debitedCurrency);
case 'creditedamount':
    return formatReimbursementAmount(report.creditedAmount, report.creditedCurrency);
```

Same latest-error extraction logic is duplicated here as in the other three bank-connection screens. Extract it into one shared helper (e.g. `getLatestErrorMessage(errors)`) and reuse it to avoid drift.

```ts
const errorMessage = getLatestErrorMessage(addNewCard?.errors ?? (newFeed ? cardFeeds?.[newFeed]?.errors : undefined));
const isNewFeedHasError = !!errorMessage;
```

This latest-error extraction duplicates the same logic added in the personal-card and native company-card bank-connection screens. Move it to a shared helper so all four call sites stay consistent.

```ts
const errorMessage = getLatestErrorMessage(addNewCard?.errors ?? (newFeed ? cardFeeds?.[newFeed]?.errors : undefined));
const isNewFeedHasError = !!errorMessage;
```

The logic to extract the latest error string is duplicated verbatim across four bank-connection screens (this file plus `index.native.tsx`, and both `companyCards/BankConnection/index.*`). Duplicated logic increases maintenance cost and bug risk.

Extract this into a shared helper and reuse it everywhere:

```ts
// e.g. in a shared util
function getLatestErrorMessage(errors?: OnyxCommon.Errors): string | undefined {
    const latestError = Object.values(errors ?? {}).at(-1);
    return typeof latestError === 'string' ? latestError : undefined;
}
```

Then: `const errorMessage = getLatestErrorMessage(addNewCard?.errors);`

This exact `showConfirmModal({title: 'bankAccount.unlockAlreadyRequestedTitle', prompt: 'bankAccount.unlockAlreadyRequestedDescription', confirmText: 'common.buttonConfirm', shouldShowCancelButton: false})` block is duplicated verbatim in four places: here, `UnlockBankAccount.tsx`, `WalletPage/index.tsx`, and `WorkflowsPaymentsTab.tsx`. Duplicated logic like this raises maintenance overhead and drifts out of sync.

Extract the modal into a shared helper (e.g. a `showUnlockAlreadyRequestedModal(showConfirmModal, translate)` util or a small hook) and call it from each of the four sites:

```tsx
function showUnlockAlreadyRequestedModal(showConfirmModal, translate) {
    showConfirmModal({
        title: translate('bankAccount.unlockAlreadyRequestedTitle'),
        prompt: translate('bankAccount.unlockAlreadyRequestedDescription'),
        confirmText: translate('common.buttonConfirm'),
        shouldShowCancelButton: false,
