This inline computation duplicates the exact seeded-avatar resolution that the same PR is centralizing inside `buildUserIcon` (`personalDetails.avatar ?? getDefaultAvatarURL({accountID, accountEmail: addSMSDomainIfPhoneNumber(login)})`). `UserAvatarUtils.ts` even documents `buildUserIcon` as the place where "the resolution stays in one place", so replicating the same `getDefaultAvatarURL({accountID, accountEmail: addSMSDomainIfPhoneNumber(memberLogin)})` fallback here re-implements shared logic and risks the two copies drifting apart.

Resolve the avatar through the shared path instead of hand-rolling the fallback. For example, derive the icon via `buildUserIcon`/`useAccountIcons` (passing `accountEmail: memberLogin`) and pass its `source`, or extract a small helper in `UserAvatarUtils.ts` (e.g. `getSeededAvatarSource(accountID, login)`) and call it in both places:

```tsx
// UserAvatarUtils.ts
function getSeededAvatarSource(accountID: number, accountEmail?: string): AvatarSource | undefined {
    return accountEmail ? getDefaultAvatarURL({accountID, accountEmail: addSMSDomainIfPhoneNumber(accountEmail)}) : undefined;
}

// WorkspaceMemberDetailsPage.tsx
const memberAvatarSource = details.avatar ?? getSeededAvatarSource(accountID, memberLogin);
```

The new `getFeedWithoutDomainID` duplicates the split-on-separator, take-first-segment logic already implemented by `getCompanyCardFeed` in `@libs/CardUtils` (which this PR replaces here). Both split on `CONST.COMPANY_CARD.FEED_KEY_SEPARATOR` and return the first segment. Maintaining two copies of the same feed-parsing rule risks them drifting apart.

Consider sharing a single implementation. For example, have the base parser return a plain string and let the typed variant wrap it:

```ts
// in CardUtils.ts
function getFeedWithoutDomainID(feed: string): string {
    const [feedWithoutDomainID] = feed.split(CONST.COMPANY_CARD.FEED_KEY_SEPARATOR);
    return feedWithoutDomainID;
}

function getCompanyCardFeed(feedWithDomainID: CardFeedWithNumber | CardFeedWithDomainID | undefined): CompanyCardFeedWithNumber {
    if (!feedWithDomainID) {
        return '' as CompanyCardFeedWithNumber;
    }
    return getFeedWithoutDomainID(feedWithDomainID) as CompanyCardFeedWithNumber;
}
```

Then import the shared `getFeedWithoutDomainID` in `Plaid.ts` instead of redefining it.

The cross-policy rate resolution loop here (try the passed `policy` first, then iterate every policy in `policies` looking up `getRateByCustomUnitRateID`) duplicates the existing exported helper `DistanceRequestUtils.getEnabledRateByCustomUnitRateIDFromAnyPolicy`. That helper is already used with the exact same "specific policy first, then any policy" fallback pattern in this same file (`TransactionUtils/index.ts:826`) and in `SplitExpenseItems.ts:192` (`getRateByCustomUnitRateID(...) ?? getEnabledRateByCustomUnitRateIDFromAnyPolicy(...)`).

Reuse the existing helper instead of reimplementing the loop:

```ts
function getDistanceRateForDisplay({customUnitRateID, policy, policies}: {customUnitRateID: string | undefined; policy?: OnyxEntry<Policy>; policies?: OnyxCollection<Policy>}) {
    if (!customUnitRateID) {
        return;
    }
    return DistanceRequestUtils.getRateByCustomUnitRateID({customUnitRateID, policy}) ?? DistanceRequestUtils.getEnabledRateByCustomUnitRateIDFromAnyPolicy(customUnitRateID, policies);
}
```

This whole module is a near-verbatim copy of `src/libs/ExpensifyCardFeedSelectorUtils.ts`. `isTravelBillingFeedVisibleToAdmin`, `isFeedPrimaryForPolicy`, `getAdminTravelBillingFeedEntries` and `partitionTravelBillingFeedsForSelector` are structurally identical to `isExpensifyCardFeedVisibleToAdmin`, `isFeedPrimaryForPolicy`, `getAdminExpensifyCardFeedEntries` and `partitionExpensifyCardFeedsForSelector`. The only real difference is which program block `hasConfiguredTravelBillingFeed` inspects (`CONST.TRAVEL.PROGRAM_TRAVEL_US`) versus `hasConfiguredExpensifyCardFeed` (`US`/`GB`). Duplicating this logic means any future fix (e.g. to the admin-visibility rule) must be applied in two places and can silently drift.

Consider parameterizing the existing helpers by the program-block predicate instead of copying them, e.g.:

```ts
// In ExpensifyCardFeedSelectorUtils.ts
function getAdminFeedEntries(
    cardSettingsCollection: OnyxCollection<ExpensifyCardSettings>,
    policies: OnyxCollection<Policy>,
    domains: OnyxCollection<Domain>,
    currentUserAccountID: number,
    hasConfiguredFeed: (settings: ExpensifyCardSettings | undefined) => boolean,
) { /* shared implementation */ }

// Travel just supplies its own predicate
const hasConfiguredTravelBillingFeed = (settings) => { /* TRAVEL_US check */ };
```

so the visibility/partition logic lives in one place and travel only provides the program-specific predicate.

The logic mapping `walletProvider` (`APPLE_PAY` / `ANDROID_PAY` / fallback) to a wallet display name is duplicated verbatim here and in `src/pages/home/TimeSensitiveSection/items/ConfirmDigitalWalletAddition.tsx` (lines 25-31). Only the translation-key prefix differs, so any change to the provider mapping must be made in two places.

Extract a shared helper (e.g. in `CardUtils`) that returns the correct wallet-name translation key for a given `walletProvider`, and call it from both files:

```ts
function getWalletProviderNameKey(walletProvider?: ValueOf<typeof CONST.EXPENSIFY_CARD.WALLET_PROVIDER>) {
    if (walletProvider === CONST.EXPENSIFY_CARD.WALLET_PROVIDER.APPLE_PAY) {
        return 'appleWallet';
    }
    if (walletProvider === CONST.EXPENSIFY_CARD.WALLET_PROVIDER.ANDROID_PAY) {
        return 'googleWallet';
    }
    return 'digitalWallet';
}
```

This inlined border-radius branch (`shape === ROUNDED_SQUARE ? avatarBorderSizes[size] : variables.buttonBorderRadius`) duplicates the exact logic already living in `getAvatarBorderRadius`, which is still used elsewhere (e.g. `HorizontalAvatars`, `DiagonalAvatars`). Now the same visual rule is computed in two places keyed on `shape` vs `type`, so a future change to avatar corner rounding must be made twice.

Consider consolidating so `getAvatarBorderStyle` delegates to a single source of truth, e.g. keep `getAvatarBorderRadius` as the one implementation and have `getAvatarBorderStyle` map `shape` to it:

```ts
function getAvatarBorderStyle(size: AvatarSizeName, shape: AvatarShape): ViewStyle {
    return {
        overflow: 'hidden',
        borderRadius: shape === CONST.AVATAR_SHAPE.ROUNDED_SQUARE ? avatarBorderSizes[size] : variables.buttonBorderRadius,
    };
}
// ...and update getAvatarBorderRadius to share the same helper, avoiding two divergent copies of the rule.
```

This `isTravelEnabled` derivation (lines 116-123) is a verbatim copy of the same logic in `src/pages/inbox/sidebar/FABPopoverContent/menuItems/TravelMenuItem.tsx` (lines 33-42): the same `isBlockedFromSpotnanaTravel` beta check, `primaryContactMethod` fallback, `Str.isSMSLogin` guard, `isPaidGroupPolicy`, and `hasAcceptedTravelTerms` calls, reading the identical Onyx values (`primaryLogin`, `sessionEmail`, `allBetas`, `activePolicy`, `travelSettings`). The `openBookTravel` function (lines 80-87) is likewise a copy of `TravelMenuItem`'s `openTravel`. Duplicating this eligibility logic in two places means any future change to Travel-booking rules must be made in both, and they will silently drift apart.

Extract the shared logic into a reusable hook (e.g. `useTravelBooking` returning `{isTravelVisible, isTravelEnabled, openBookTravel}`) and consume it from both `TravelMenuItem` and `useCreateNavigationSuggestions`:

```ts
// src/hooks/useTravelBooking.ts
function useTravelBooking() {
    const [activePolicyID] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.NVP_ACTIVE_POLICY_ID);
    const [activePolicy] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.POLICY}${activePolicyID}`);
    const [travelSettings] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.NVP_TRAVEL_SETTINGS);
    const [primaryLogin] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.ACCOUNT, {selector: primaryLoginSelector});
    const [sessionEmail] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.SESSION, {selector: emailSelector});
    const [allBetas] = useOnyx(ONYXKEYS.BETAS);
    const primaryContactMethod = primaryLogin ?? sessionEmail ?? '';
    const isTravelEnabled =
        !Permissions.isBetaEnabled(CONST.BETAS.PREVENT_SPOTNANA_TRAVEL, allBetas) &&
        !!primaryContactMethod &&
        !Str.isSMSLogin(primaryContactMethod) &&
        isPaidGroupPolicy(activePolicy) &&
        hasAcceptedTravelTerms(activePolicy, travelSettings);
    const openBookTravel = () => (isTravelEnabled ? openTravelDotLink(activePolicy?.id) : Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.TRAVEL_MY_TRIPS.getRoute(activePolicy?.id)));
    return {isTravelVisible: !!activePolicy?.isTravelEnabled, isTravelEnabled, openBookTravel};
}
```

This inlines the `Str.removeSMSDomain(name ? name : (email ?? ''))` fallback that the removed `getUserNameByEmail` helper used to encapsulate. The same expression now appears here and in three other places (line 107 below, `CardNameStep.tsx`, and `ConfirmationStep.tsx`), duplicating the name-resolution logic that was previously in a single shared function.

Extract this into one reusable helper (a pure function that takes the personal detail and the fallback email, or a small `useUserNameByLogin` hook wrapping `usePersonalDetailByLogin`) and call it from all sites instead of repeating the ternary + `removeSMSDomain` pattern:

```ts
function getUserNameFromDetail(name: string | undefined, fallbackEmail: string): string {
    return Str.removeSMSDomain(name ? name : fallbackEmail);
}
```

The success, wrong-account, and failure branches each repeat the same `<ScrollView contentContainerStyle={styles.flexGrow1}>` + `<ConfirmationPage ... shouldShowButton onButtonPress={() => Navigation.goBack(backPath)} buttonText={translate('common.buttonConfirm')} containerStyle={styles.h100} />` scaffold verbatim, differing only in heading/description/illustration. This triples the surface area to maintain.

Extract a small local helper that renders the shared scaffold and takes the varying parts as arguments, e.g.:

```tsx
const renderResult = (heading: string, illustration: IconAsset, descriptionProps: {description?: string; descriptionComponent?: ReactNode}) => (
    <ScrollView contentContainerStyle={styles.flexGrow1}>
        <ConfirmationPage
            heading={heading}
            illustration={illustration}
            descriptionStyle={styles.textSupportingNormal}
            shouldShowButton
            onButtonPress={() => Navigation.goBack(backPath)}
            buttonText={translate('common.buttonConfirm')}
