The boundary clamping logic (lines 389-418 in `createEdgeGesture` and lines 478-507 in `createCornerGesture`) is duplicated verbatim. Both gesture handlers contain the same ~30 lines of code for clamping the crop rectangle within image bounds, setting shared values, computing the `CropRect`, and calling `onCropChange`.

Extract the shared logic into a helper function, e.g.:

```ts
const applyCropBoundsAndNotify = useCallback((newX: number, newY: number, newWidth: number, newHeight: number, imageLeft: number, imageRight: number, imageTop: number, imageBottom: number) => {
    // clamp to image bounds
    let x = newX, y = newY, w = newWidth, h = newHeight;
    if (x < imageLeft) { const diff = imageLeft - x; x = imageLeft; w -= diff; }
    if (x + w > imageRight) { w = imageRight - x; }
    if (y < imageTop) { const diff = imageTop - y; y = imageTop; h -= diff; }
    if (y + h > imageBottom) { h = imageBottom - y; }

    cropX.set(x); cropY.set(y);
    cropWidth.set(w); cropHeight.set(h);

    onCropChange?.({
        x: (x - imageOffsetX) * scaleX,
        y: (y - imageOffsetY) * scaleY,
        width: w * scaleX,
        height: h * scaleY,
    });
}, [cropX, cropY, cropWidth, cropHeight, imageOffsetX, scaleX, imageOffsetY, scaleY, onCropChange]);
```

Then call `applyCropBoundsAndNotify(newX, newY, newWidth, newHeight, imageLeft, imageRight, imageTop, imageBottom)` in both gesture handlers.

